  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0184 database is designed to manage information about users, restaurants, food items, customer service interactions, and orders. The User table stores details about each individual, including their phone number, password, email, name, date of birth, and address. The Food table contains information about the various food items available, including their name, price, availability, rating, quantity, and description. The Restaurant table holds details about each restaurant, such as its name, founding date, address, rating, and description. The CustomerService table tracks customer inquiries, including the email and name of the customer, the inquiry itself, its status, and the date it was submitted. The OrderHistory table stores information about each order, including the user who made it, the total price, and the date it was placed, referencing the User table for the user's details. The Products table links users to the orders they have made, referencing both the User and OrderHistory tables. The Interacts table tracks interactions between customers and customer service, referencing both the CustomerService and User tables. The Buys table shows which users have purchased which food items, referencing both the User and Food tables. Finally, the Sells table tracks which restaurants sell which food items, referencing both the Restaurant and Food tables.",
  "gold_question": "Background / Context\n\nTunisia faces a significant issue with food waste, with average food waste per family amounting to 5% of total food expenditure. The UN Food and Agriculture Organization reports high annual rates of food waste globally, impacting resources and economies. In Tunisia, this waste contributes to local shortages and hinders access to affordable food for low-income populations.\n\nObjectives\n\nThe objective is to develop a MySQL-based implementation of EcoEats, a MERN stack web application designed to manage food waste. The platform will enable businesses such as hotels, bakeries, restaurants, and supermarkets to sell excess food items at reduced prices, aiming to minimize food waste and provide affordable food options.\n\nStakeholders & Users\n\n- Stakeholders: Business owners (hotels, restaurants, bakeries, supermarkets), platform administrators, government agencies focused on food waste reduction.\n- Users: Low-income individuals, general customers looking for affordable food options, customer service representatives.\n\nFunctional Descriptions\n\n1. User Management:\n   - Registration and authentication of users with personal details, including contact information and addresses.\n   - Users can update personal information and view past order history.\n\n2. Product Management:\n   - Businesses can list excess food items with details such as name, price, availability, and description.\n   - Products can be associated with specific restaurants or sellers.\n\n3. Ordering System:\n   - Users can browse the available food items, place orders, and make secure payments.\n   - System to track order histories and manage purchases.\n\n4. Customer Service:\n   - Provide a platform for user inquiries, managed by customer service representatives.\n   - Track status and respond to customer service inquiries.\n\n5. Interactions with Businesses:\n   - Facilitate interaction between users and the businesses selling food items.\n   - Manage records of products sold by businesses.\n\nNon-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Performance: The system should support seamless interactions and manage a significant number of concurrent users.\n- Scalability: The platform should be able to scale with an increase in the number of users and transactions.\n- Security: Ensure secure handling of user data and payment transactions.\n- Usability: The platform should be user-friendly, accessible, and intuitive for both sellers and buyers.\n- Reliability: The application should consistently function as intended with minimal downtime.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_llama31/0184.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0184\nCREATE TABLE `user` (\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `phone_number` varchar(15) NOT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `date_of_birth` date NOT NULL,\n  `address` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`user_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `food` (\n  `food_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `price` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `availability` boolean NOT NULL DEFAULT TRUE,\n  `rating` float NOT NULL,\n  `quantity` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`food_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `restaurant` (\n  `restaurant_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `founding_date` date NOT NULL,\n  `address` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `rating` float NOT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`restaurant_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `customer_service` (\n  `inquiry_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `customer_email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `customer_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `inquiry` text NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `date_submitted` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`inquiry_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `order_history` (\n  `order_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `total_price` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `date_placed` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`order_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `order_history_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `user` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `products` (\n  `product_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `order_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`product_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `products_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `user` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `products_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`order_id`) REFERENCES `order_history` (`order_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `interacts` (\n  `interaction_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `customer_service_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`interaction_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `interacts_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`customer_service_id`) REFERENCES `customer_service` (`inquiry_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `interacts_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `user` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `buys` (\n  `buy_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `food_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`buy_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `buys_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `user` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `buys_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`food_id`) REFERENCES `food` (`food_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `sells` (\n  `sell_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `restaurant_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `food_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`sell_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `sells_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`restaurant_id`) REFERENCES `restaurant` (`restaurant_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `sells_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`food_id`) REFERENCES `food` (`food_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
